# Enterprise Business Analytics & Forecasting Platform

A production-style analytics platform built end-to-end: raw data → ETL →
PostgreSQL star schema → SQL analytics → statistics → machine learning →
Power BI / Excel → executive reporting. Built as a guided learning project
covering the full Data Analyst / Analytics Engineer / BI Developer / Junior
Data Scientist toolkit.

POWER.BI DASHBOARD



## Tech Stack

Python (pandas, numpy, sqlalchemy, scipy, statsmodels, scikit-learn,
xgboost) · PostgreSQL · SQL (CTEs, window functions, views) · Power BI
(DAX) · Excel (Power Query, PivotTables, forecasting functions)

## Architecture

```
enterprise-analytics-platform/
├── data/
│   └── raw/              # source CSVs (Bronze layer)
├── etl/                  # extract.py, transform.py, load.py, pipeline.py
├── sql/
│   ├── schema/           # table + index DDL
│   ├── analysis/         # ad-hoc analytical queries
│   └── views/            # permanent reusable business views
├── analytics/            # kpis.py, business_stats.py
├── ml/                   # features.py, train.py, classify.py
├── powerbi/              # SETUP_GUIDE.md, dax_measures.dax
├── excel/                # sales_workbook.xlsx
├── reports/               # executive_summary/ (Word reports)
├── config/               # config.py (centralized, .env-based settings)
├── .env.example           # committed credential template
├── requirements.txt
└── README.md
```

Data flows in one direction: raw files are read by `etl/`, loaded into
PostgreSQL's star schema (`fact_sales` + four dimension tables), then
consumed independently by SQL analytics, Python statistics/ML, and BI
tools — no downstream consumer writes back upstream.

**Why the star schema, not a fully normalized OLTP schema:** this platform
is built for analytical reads (OLAP), not high-frequency transactional
writes — see Module 3 for the full reasoning.

**Why full-refresh, not incremental load:** `etl/pipeline.py` truncates
and reloads `fact_sales` on every run rather than tracking incrementally
loaded records. This is the right tradeoff at our current data volume;
it will not scale to a very large, continuously-growing fact table
without revisiting this design (see Module 4, Part 6).

## Setup (Windows)

1. Install Python 3.11+, PostgreSQL + pgAdmin, Git, VS Code.
2. `python -m venv venv` then `venv\Scripts\activate`
3. `pip install -r requirements.txt`
4. Create a database named `enterprise_analytics` in pgAdmin.
5. Run `sql/schema/01_create_tables.sql` then `02_create_indexes.sql` in
   pgAdmin's Query Tool.
6. Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and fill in your real PostgreSQL
   credentials.
7. Run `python etl/pipeline.py` to load the sample dataset.
8. Run `python analytics/kpis.py`, `python analytics/business_stats.py`,
   `python ml/train.py`, and `python ml/classify.py` to reproduce all
   analysis.
9. For Power BI / Excel: see `powerbi/SETUP_GUIDE.md` and Module 10.

## Module Map

| Module | What it built |
|---|---|
| 1 | Dev environment: Python, VS Code, Git, PostgreSQL, venv |
| 2 | Project architecture, folder structure, data flow design |
| 3 | Star schema design, indexes |
| 4 | ETL pipeline: extract, transform, load, orchestration |
| 5 | SQL analytics: aggregations, window functions, CTEs, views |
| 6 | Business KPIs: revenue, profit, CLV, growth metrics |
| 7 | Statistics: correlation, hypothesis testing, regression, ARIMA |
| 8 | Machine learning: regression, classification, feature importance |
| 9 | Power BI: data model, DAX, dashboards, publishing |
| 10 | Excel: PivotTables, Power Query, forecasting |
| 11 | Executive reporting, this README |


Learning project. Not connected to a real production data source.
